#
# Conformance case: teleology/strong-cyclic-plan.
#
# A plan over a two-branch nondeterministic outcome set where one branch reaches
# the goal directly (successOut → goalSit) and one branch produces a transient
# failure (transientFailOut → retrySit) that is recoverable (recoverableOutcome
# "true").  Every fair execution eventually reaches goalSit via the retry loop,
# so the evaluator must emit StrongCyclicPlanSuccess, NOT WeakPlanSuccess (which
# would require at least one non-recoverable non-reaching outcome) and NOT
# StrongPlanSuccess (which would require EVERY outcome to reach the goal).
#
# The SAME plan also carries a reified control-flow graph (logic:planFlowEdge:
# probe → recover → probe is a retry back-edge, plus probe → commit on success) and
# runs logic:executedUnderContract logic:DagWorkflowContract. So the plan is valid
# CANONICALLY (StrongCyclicPlanSuccess) yet resolves to `unsupported` under the
# DAG-workflow profile: the evaluator emits a logic:ReasoningResult with
# logic:resultEvaluation logic:EvaluationUnsupported and discloses the offending
# cycle members (probe, recover) through logic:dagCycleWitness — never silently
# truncated — and the run's preservation claim turns SoundUnder, naming the dropped
# loop. The acyclicity verdict comes from the SAME shared certifier the build
# pipeline delegates to (gmeow_logic::dag_profile::certify_acyclic).
#
# CRITICAL: the teleology evaluator seeds ONLY from input.nq world facts; this
# input.logic.ttl carries the minimal, clean-compiling, certifiable program axiom
# the projections need (no asserted teleology triples here — the world facts live in
# input.nq, exactly as the foundation conformance cases keep theirs).

@prefix logic: <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/logic/> .
@prefix ex:    <https://example.org/teleology/strong-cyclic-plan/> .

ex:GoalExpression a logic:GoalExpression .
